# gate.steps — the certification gate of this repository (gate/DESIGN.md "The tree owns its
# gate", sp-quu2w). The gate reads THIS file from the tree it judges — the branch merged onto
# its landing ref — and the base trial reads the landing ref's own copy. Production config
# holds no gate string: a branch that ports, adds or deletes a fence edits this file in the
# same commit, and its own gate runs exactly what it would land with.
#
# One directive per line, so a fence port is a one-line diff:
#   step <command>          one element of the `&&` chain, in order
#   bin <VAR> <package>     build <package> from this tree (cargo, aeon profile) before any
#                           step, and give the steps $VAR = that binary instead of the
#                           installed one — so a rule a branch adds to it runs on that branch.
#                           This is BUILDING a tree under test, so it is passed by explicit
#                           variable; every other tool a step uses is the running release's,
#                           invoked by bare name on the launcher's PATH (sp-gypjk)
#   # comment, blank line   ignored
#
# Every `step bash <path>` must name a file this tree carries (the gate refuses the branch
# otherwise, and `cargo test -p gate` checks it). Every fence prints
# `fence: <name> checked <n> <unit>` with n > 0 (DESIGN.md "Every fence proves it checked").
# A unit composition drops the `step bash spira/build-fence.sh` line (its build phase is the
# compile check, sp-aprxm); keep it a line of its own.

bin SPIRA_LINT_BIN spira-lint
bin SPIRA_SELECT_BIN suite-select
bin SPIRA_LIFECYCLE_GUARD_BIN lifecycle-guard
# The suite runner is the tree's own (sp-isom7): how a tree's suites are staged and launched —
# the release layout testenv builds in the container and the PATH it sets from it — is part of
# the tree under test, so a branch that changes it is judged by it. The release's testenv could
# not run such a branch's suites at all (the first one to need this was sp-isom7 itself).
bin SPIRA_TESTENV_BIN testenv

# inventory, literal-lint, scratch-fence, tmux-scope-fence and wiki-add-fence
# (sp-ekkak), and testdb-mode-lint, bd-stdin-lint and incident-cause-lint, moved to
# spira-lint rules of the same name; the bash scripts are deleted and their checks now run
# as part of the "$SPIRA_LINT_BIN" step below. gh-intake-lint (also sp-ekkak) was one of
# these but is itself now retired (sp-8fsql): its subject, spira/gh-intake.sh, is deleted,
# and the property it scanned for is structural in the gh-intake crate that replaced it.
step "$SPIRA_LINT_BIN"
step bash spira/build-fence.sh

# The lifecycle analyser (sp-ece5q) with an empty allowlist: no --rules file, so a change that
# reaches lifecycle state by any path other than the machine fails the gate.
step "$SPIRA_LIFECYCLE_GUARD_BIN" .

# The suite selector (suite-select, sp-wx2tw) and the budgeted suite run. The selector's exit 1
# is an unclaimed source file (the branch's fault: FAIL); any other failure is a refusal
# (NO_VERDICT) — an empty selection from a broken selector is not "nothing to run".
step { _s="$("$SPIRA_SELECT_BIN" gate "$SPIRA_GATE_BASE" "${SPIRA_GATE_SELECT_HEAD:-$SPIRA_GATE_BRANCH}")" || { _r=$?; [ "$_r" = 1 ] && exit 1; exit 75; }; [ -n "$_s" ] || exit 0; _b=0; "$SPIRA_TESTENV_BIN" --deadline "${SPIRA_GATE_BUDGET:-300}" --suites "${_s//$'\n'/,}" "$SPIRA_GATE_BRANCH" "$SPIRA_GATE_REPO" || _b=$?; case "$_b" in 2|3|127) exit 75;; *) exit "$_b";; esac; }
